            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that the order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Spiral similarity with centre \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the two circles intersect, there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that maps \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\in\\Omega\\) we denote by \\(X^{\\mathcal S}\\) its image. By construction\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\mathcal S(M)=N,\\qquad \\mathcal S(C)=D,\\\\\n&\\text{for every }X\\in\\Omega,\\; A,X,X^{\\mathcal S}\\text{ are collinear, and }AX^{\\mathcal S}= \\frac{R}{r}\\,AX .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nApplying \\(\\mathcal S\\) to the point \\(E\\in\\Omega\\) yields the point \\(F\\in\\Gamma\\); thus \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear and \\(AP\\) coincides with the line \\(AEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. The circle \\((ACD)\\)\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Quadrilateral \\(A M P N\\) is cyclic\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\); consequently \\(CD\\perp MN\\).  \nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) passes through the midpoint of \\(MN\\) and through \\(P\\) (because \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\)). Hence \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\); in particular\n\n\\[\n\\angle AMP = \\angle ANP = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThus the opposite angles of quadrilateral \\(A M P N\\) are equal, and therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{A,M,P,N\\text{ are concyclic}} . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nFrom the cyclicity we obtain the equality of the subtended arcs\n\n\\[\n\\angle MAP = \\angle NAP . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBy definition of orthocenter,\n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN,\\qquad MH\\perp PN,\\qquad NH\\perp PM . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(PH\\) is the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. The altitude \\(PH\\) bisects \\(\\angle MPN\\)\n\nIn any triangle the internal bisector of an angle consists of the points that are equidistant from the two sides forming the angle.  \nBecause \\(PH\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and the reflection in the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) interchanges \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) while fixing \\(P\\), every point of the line \\(PH\\) is at the same distance from the lines \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{PH\\text{ is the internal bisector of }\\angle MPN}. \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\n\\angle MPH = \\angle HPN . \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Angle relation supplied by the spiral similarity\n\nThe spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes the common chord \\(AB\\) of the two circles and sends the point \\(E\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) to the point \\(F\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\). Consequently the image of the angle \\(\\angle AEB\\) under \\(\\mathcal S\\) is \\(\\angle BFA\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle BFA . \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear, \\(\\angle BFA = \\angle BFE\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle AEB = \\angle BFE}. \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. Construction of the required tangent\n\nLet \\(L\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). Because \\(AP\\) contains the collinear points \\(A,E,F\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle(L,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle AEB . \\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nUsing (18) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\angle(L,BE)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nEquality (20) is exactly the tangency condition for a line to a circle: the angle formed by the line with a chord equals the angle subtended by that chord in the opposite arc. Therefore \\(L\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8. Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) satisfies the tangency condition (20); consequently it is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
