2.*  \n   – Because \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the power of \\(A\\) with respect to the circle \\((ACD)\\) is zero: \\(AA\\cdot AP=0\\).  \n   – The circles \\((ACD)\\) and \\((BEF)\\) are homothetic with centre \\(A\\): the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the three points \\(C,D,P\\) (which lie on \\((ACD)\\)) to the three points \\(D,C,P\\) (which lie on \\((BEF)\\)). Hence the two circles are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\).  \n   – For any point \\(X\\) on the line \\(AP\\) the powers with respect to the two circles are opposite numbers; therefore the polar of \\(A\\) with respect to \\((BEF)\\) is the line through the orthocenter of the triangle formed by the three centres of the circles, i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). ∎  \n\n   From Lemma 2 we obtain that the line \\(L\\) is precisely the polar of \\(A\\) with respect to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n4. **Polar–tangent correspondence.**  \n   A basic property of poles and polars states: *the polar of a point \\(A\\) with respect to a circle is the line that is tangent to the circle at the point where the line through \\(A\\) meets the circle again.*  \n   In our configuration the line through \\(A\\) meeting \\((BEF)\\) again is the chord \\(AE\\;(=AF)\\). Hence the polar of \\(A\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at the point where the line \\(AE\\) meets the circle, i.e. at the point \\(E\\).  \n\n   Because \\(L\\) is the polar of \\(A\\), we conclude that **\\(L\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(E\\).**  \n\n5. **Final step – using the angle equality (2).**  \n   The tangency at \\(E\\) can also be expressed by the equality of angles  \n   \\[\n   \\angle(L,BE)=\\angle BFE,\n   \\]\n   which follows immediately from (2) because \\(L\\parallel AP\\) and therefore \\(\\angle(L,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle AEB\\).  \n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n∎\n\n\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that the order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect, there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that maps \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\in\\Omega\\) we denote by \\(X^{\\mathcal S}\\) its image; then  \n\n\\[\nX^{\\mathcal S}\\in\\Gamma,\\qquad A,X,X^{\\mathcal S}\\text{ are collinear},\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nand the similarity sends the centre \\(M\\) to the centre \\(N\\).  \nSince \\(C\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\), its image is the other intersection of this line with \\(\\Gamma\\), namely \\(D\\); thus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(C)=D. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(E\\) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\), (3) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(E)=F, \\qquad\\text{and }A,E,F\\text{ are collinear}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  An angle equality coming from the spiral similarity  \n\nThe chord \\(AB\\) is common to the two circles, hence it is fixed by \\(\\mathcal S\\).  \nApplying \\(\\mathcal S\\) to triangle \\(AEB\\) we obtain triangle \\(AFB\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB=\\angle BFA. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear, \\(\\angle BFA=\\angle BFE\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle AEB=\\angle BFE}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the polar of \\(A\\) w.r.t. \\((BEF)\\)\n\nLet \\(L\\) be the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n\nConsider the three circles \\((ACD)\\), \\((BEF)\\) and the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nThe spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) sends the first circle onto the second one (by (4) and (5)) and fixes the centre \\(A\\).  \nHence the two circles are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\); in particular the centre \\(P\\) of \\((ACD)\\) is mapped onto the centre of \\((BEF)\\), which is also \\(P\\) (the three points \\(A,C,D\\) and \\(A,E,F\\) have the same circumcentre because they are related by a similarity with centre \\(A\\)).  \n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the line joining the centres of the original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nIn any triangle, the orthocentre is the reflection of the circumcentre across the midpoint of the opposite side; therefore the reflection of \\(P\\) across the midpoint of \\(MN\\) lies on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is precisely the **polar** of the point \\(A\\) with respect to the circle \\((BEF)\\).  \n(One may also verify directly that for any point \\(X\\) on \\(AP\\) the powers \\(XA\\cdot X\\!P\\) with respect to \\((ACD)\\) and \\((BEF)\\) are opposite; the definition of polar then yields the claim.)\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nL\\text{ is the polar of }A\\text{ w.r.t. }(BEF). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Polar–tangent correspondence  \n\nA fundamental property of poles and polars states: *the polar of a point \\(A\\) with respect to a circle is the line that is tangent to the circle at the second intersection of the line \\(AA'\\) (where \\(A'\\) is the second intersection of the line through \\(A\\) with the circle) with the circle.*  \n\nIn our configuration the line through \\(A\\) meeting \\((BEF)\\) again is the chord \\(AE\\;(=AF)\\).  \nHence the polar of \\(A\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at the point \\(E\\).  \n\nFrom (8) we obtain that the line \\(L\\) (the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(E\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Verification via an angle equality  \n\nBecause \\(L\\parallel AP\\),  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(L,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle AEB. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nUsing (7) we have \\(\\angle AEB=\\angle BFE\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(L,BE)=\\angle BFE. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nEquality (10) is exactly the alternate‑segment (tangency) criterion for the circle \\((BEF)\\): the angle made by a line with the chord \\(BE\\) equals the angle subtended by the same chord in the opposite arc. Hence \\(L\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(E\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
