Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have not changed the statement that  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle AEB=\\angle BFA\\;}\n\\]\n\n(which is correct).  \nWhat was wrong in the previous write‑up is the claim that the line \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((AEB)\\).  \nIn the revised proof I replace that claim by a direct angle‑chasing argument which shows  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFE .\n\\]\n\nBy the alternate‑segment (tangency) criterion this is exactly the condition that the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Spiral similarity at \\(A\\).**  \n   The two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\)) admit a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that carries \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n   *Lemma 1.*  For any point \\(X\\in\\Omega\\) the image \\(X^{\\mathcal S}\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\) and on the line \\(AX\\). In particular \\(E^{\\mathcal S}=F\\).\n\n2. **Angle equality supplied by the spiral similarity.**  \n   Because the common chord \\(AB\\) is fixed by \\(\\mathcal S\\), applying \\(\\mathcal S\\) to \\(\\triangle AEB\\) gives \\(\\triangle AFB\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\angle AEB=\\angle BFA}\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   and, since \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear,  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\angle AEB=\\angle BFE}\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **A relation between the directions of \\(AP\\) and \\(AB\\).**  \n   *Lemma 2.*  In the configuration described, the line \\(AB\\) is parallel to the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\); i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   AB\\;\\parallel\\;PH .\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  The line \\(MN\\) joins the centres of the two intersecting circles, hence it is perpendicular to the common chord \\(AB\\).  \n   Since \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(PC=PD\\); therefore \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(CD\\), which is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  Consequently the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) is a line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\), and the same is true for \\(AB\\).  Hence \\(AB\\parallel PH\\). ∎\n\n4. **The crucial angle equality.**  \n   Because \\(AB\\parallel PH\\) and \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) makes with \\(AB\\) exactly the same angle as the line \\(AP\\) makes with \\(AB\\).  Therefore it suffices to prove  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\angle (AP,AB)=\\angle AEB}\\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   (the left‑hand side is the oriented angle from \\(AP\\) to \\(AB\\)).  \n\n   *Lemma 3.*  In the circle \\((ACD)\\) with centre \\(P\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP,AB)=\\tfrac12\\angle CPD .\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  In the circle \\((ACD)\\) the central angle \\(\\angle CPD\\) subtends the same chord \\(CD\\) as the inscribed angle \\(\\angle CAD\\); hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CPD = 2\\angle CAD . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The quadrilateral \\(ABMN\\) is a kite with \\(AB\\perp MN\\); consequently the line \\(AB\\) is the external angle bisector of \\(\\angle CPD\\) (the two equal angles \\(\\angle CPA\\) and \\(\\angle APD\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(AB\\)).  Therefore the oriented angle from \\(AP\\) to \\(AB\\) equals half of \\(\\angle CPD\\), i.e. (3) holds. ∎\n\n   From Lemma 3 and (4) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP,AB)=\\angle CAD .\n   \\]\n\n   But the points \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) while the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) centred at \\(A\\) sends the line \\(MN\\) onto the line \\(AP\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CAD = \\angle AEB .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence (3) is proved.\n\n5. **From (3) to the required tangency.**  \n   Using (2) we replace \\(\\angle AEB\\) by \\(\\angle BFE\\) and obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle (AP,BE)=\\angle BFE . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality (5) is precisely the alternate‑segment (tangency) criterion for the circle \\((BEF)\\): a line that makes with the chord \\(BE\\) an angle equal to the angle subtended by the same chord in the opposite arc is tangent to the circle.  Therefore **any** line parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) lies on the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) by construction; consequently that line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that the order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect, there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that carries \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\in\\Omega\\) we denote by \\(X^{\\mathcal S}\\) its image; then  \n\n\\[\nX^{\\mathcal S}\\in\\Gamma,\\qquad A,X,X^{\\mathcal S}\\text{ are collinear}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, since \\(E\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\in\\Gamma\\) lie on the line \\(AP\\), (6) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(E)=F . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  An angle equality supplied by the spiral similarity\n\nThe common chord \\(AB\\) is fixed by \\(\\mathcal S\\).  Applying \\(\\mathcal S\\) to \\(\\triangle AEB\\) we obtain \\(\\triangle AFB\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle AEB=\\angle BFA}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause the points \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear, \\(\\angle BFA=\\angle BFE\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle AEB=\\angle BFE}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The line \\(AB\\) is parallel to the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) joins the centres of the intersecting circles, hence it is perpendicular to the common chord \\(AB\\):\n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(PC=PD\\); thus \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(CD\\), i.e. on the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  Hence the altitude from \\(P\\) to the side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is a line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  By (10) the same is true for \\(AB\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nAB\\parallel PH . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Relating the directions of \\(AP\\) and \\(AB\\)\n\nIn the circle \\((ACD)\\) with centre \\(P\\) the central angle