points on the line \\(MN\\) such that the order on the line is \\(C,M,N,D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(O\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle BEF\\);  \n* \\(\\ell\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nAll points are assumed to be distinct, so the configuration is non‑degenerate.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect, there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that maps \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\in\\Omega\\) we denote by \\(X^{\\mathcal S}\\) its image; then\n\n\\[\nX^{\\mathcal S}\\in\\Gamma,\\qquad A,X,X^{\\mathcal S}\\text{ are collinear}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, since \\(E\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\in\\Gamma\\) lie on the line \\(AP\\),\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(E)=F. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Angle equality supplied by the spiral similarity\n\nThe common chord \\(AB\\) is fixed by \\(\\mathcal S\\).  Applying \\(\\mathcal S\\) to \\(\\triangle AEB\\) we obtain \\(\\triangle AFB\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle AEB=\\angle BFA}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause the points \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear, \\(\\angle BFA=\\angle BFE\\); consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle AEB=\\angle BFE}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Analytic description of the configuration\n\nPlace the coordinate system so that  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0)\\;(d>0).\n\\]\n\nWrite the equations of the two circles:\n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}.\n\