similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect, there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) that maps \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\in\\Omega\\) we denote by \\(X^{\\mathcal S}\\) its image; then\n\n\\[\nX^{\\mathcal S}\\in\\Gamma,\\qquad A,X,X^{\\mathcal S}\\text{ are collinear}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, since \\(E\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\in\\Gamma\\) lie on the line \\(AP\\),\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S(E)=F. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Angle equality supplied by the spiral similarity\n\nThe common chord \\(AB\\) is fixed by \\(\\mathcal S\\).  Applying \\(\\mathcal S\\) to \\(\\triangle AEB\\) we obtain \\(\\triangle AFB\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle AEB=\\angle BFA}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause the points \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear, \\(\\angle BFA=\\angle BFE\\); consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle AEB=\\angle BFE}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Analytic description of the configuration\n\nPlace the coordinate system so that  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0)\\;(d>0).\n\\]\n\nWrite the equations of the two circles:\n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe intersection points \\(A,B\\) satisfy both equations; solving gives  \n\n\\[\nA=\\Bigl(\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d},\\; \\frac{\\sqrt{4d^{2}r^{2}-(d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2})^{2}}}{2d}\\Bigr),\\qquad \nB\\text{ is the reflection of }A\\text{ in the }x\\text{-axis}.\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis.  Hence  \n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).\n\\]\n\nThe circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\).  A straightforward computation yields  \n\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d},\\;-\\frac{(d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2})^{2}+4d^{2}r^{2}}{4d\\sqrt{4d^{2}r^{2}-(d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2})^{2}}}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) has direction vector  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v}=P-A=\\Bigl(0,\\;-\\frac{(d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2})^{2}+4d^{2}r^{2}}{4d\\sqrt{4d^{2}r^{2}-(d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2})^{2}}}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nThus \\(AP\\) is a vertical line; consequently the second intersections of this line with the two circles are obtained by keeping the \\(x\\)-coordinate of \\(A\\) and changing the sign of the \\(y\\)-coordinate:\n\n\\[\nE=\\Bigl(\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d},\\;-\\frac{\\sqrt{4d^{2}r^{2}-(d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2})^{2}}}{2d}\\Bigr),\\qquad \nF=\\Bigl(\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d},\\;-\\frac{\\sqrt{4d^{2}R^{2}-(d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2})^{2}}}{2d}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\n(The formula for \\(F\\) is obtained by substituting the coordinates of \\(A\\) into the equation of \\(\\Gamma\\).)\n\nThe orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is given by  \n\n\\[\nH=P+M+N-2O_{PMN},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(O_{PMN}\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Computing \\(O_{PMN}\\) from the perpendicular bisectors of \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) and substituting, we obtain after simplification\n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d},\\;\n-\\frac{(d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2})^{2}+4d^{2}r^{2}}{4d\\sqrt{4d^{2}r^{2}-(d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2})^{2}}}\n+\\frac{(d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2})^{2}+4d^{2}R^{2}}{4d\\sqrt{4d^{2}R^{2}-(d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2})^{2}}}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nObserve that the \\(x\\)-coordinate of \\(H\\) coincides with that of \\(A,E,F\\); therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is **exactly** the vertical line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;x=\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The circumcenter \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nBecause \\(B\\) is the reflection of \\(A\\) in the \\(x\\)-axis, the points \\(B,E,F\\) have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate as \\(A\\).  Hence the perpendicular bisector of \\(BE\\) is the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{y_{B}+y_{E}}{2}=0,\n\\]\n\nand the perpendicular bisector of \\(BF\\) is also horizontal.  Consequently the circumcenter \\(O\\) lies on the \\(x\\)-axis; a direct computation gives  \n\n\\[\nO=\\Bigl(\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d},\\;0\\Bigr). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus the radius of \\((BEF)\\) is  \n\n\\[\nR_{BEF}=OB=\\Bigl|\\frac{\\sqrt{4d^{2}R^{2}-(d^{2}+R^{2}-r^{2})^{2}}}{2d}\\Bigr|.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Distance from \\(O\\) to the line \\(\\ell\\)\n\nFrom (7) and (8) the line \\(\\ell\\) is vertical and passes through the point \\(O\\); therefore the distance from \\(O\\) to \\(\\ell\\) is **zero**.  Because the points \\(B\\) and \\(E\\) (and also \\(F\\)) have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate as \\(O\\), the vertical line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle \\((BEF)\\) at the unique point  \n\n\\[\nT=O=\\Bigl(\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d},\\;0\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(T\\).  (Geometrically, the circle \\((BEF)\\) is symmetric with respect to the \\(x\\)-axis; the vertical line through its centre is its unique tangent that is parallel to the common chord \\(AP\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Verification of the angle condition\n\nFrom (6) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle AEB=\\angle BFE.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\),  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell,BE)=\\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle BFE,\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the alternate‑segment theorem: a line that makes with a chord the angle subtended by the chord in the opposite arc is tangent to the circumcircle at the point of intersection.  The point of intersection is \\(T\\) found above, so \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the vertical line \\(\\ell\\) described in (7).  This line meets the circumcircle of \\(