>r).\n   \\]\n\n   The two intersection points are  \n\n   \\[\n   A=\\Bigl(x_{0},y_{0}\\Bigr),\\qquad \n   B=\\Bigl(x_{0},-y_{0}\\Bigr),\n   \\]\n   where  \n\n   \\[\n   x_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d},\\qquad \n   y_{0}= \\frac{\\sqrt{\\,4d^{2}r^{2}-(d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2})^{2}\\,}}{2d}>0 .\n   \\]\n\n   The points on the line \\(MN\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   C=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).\n   \\]\n\n   The circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \\(CD\\) (the vertical line \\(x=\\frac{d+R-r}{2}\\)) and of \\(AC\\); a short computation gives  \n\n   \\[\n   P=\\Bigl(x_{p},y_{p}\\Bigr),\\qquad \n   x_{p}= \\frac{d+R-r}{2},\\qquad \n   y_{p}= -\\frac{(x_{0}+r)^{2}+y_{0}^{2}}{2y_{0}} .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The direction vector of the line \\(AP\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf v =P-A=\\bigl(v_{x},v_{y}\\bigr)\n   =\\Bigl(x_{p}-x_{0},\\,y_{p}-y_{0}\\Bigr).                                   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).**  \n   Write a generic point of the line \\(AP\\) as  \n\n   \\[\n   X(t)=A+t\\mathbf v=(x_{0}+tv_{x},\\,y_{0}+tv_{y}) .\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(X(t)\\) into the equation of \\(\\Omega\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   (x_{0}+tv_{x})^{2}+(y_{0}+tv_{y})^{2}=r^{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(t=0\\) corresponds to the known intersection \\(A\\), the other root is  \n\n   \\[\n   t_{E}= -\\,\\frac{2\\,(x_{0}v_{x}+y_{0}v_{y})}{v_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}} .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   E=X(t_{E}) .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Repeating the same computation with the equation of \\(\\Gamma\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   t_{F}= -\\,\\frac{2\\bigl((x_{0}-d)v_{x}+y_{0}v_{y}\\bigr)}{v_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2}},\\qquad \n   F=X(t_{F}) .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Since \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(x=x_{p}\\).  \n   The altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through the origin perpendicular to \\(PN\\); its slope is \\(-\\dfrac{x_{p}-d}{y_{p}}\\).  \n   Intersecting the two altitudes gives  \n\n   \\[\n   H=\\Bigl(x_{p},\\;-\\frac{x_{p}(x_{p}-d)}{y_{p}}\\Bigr).\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n5. **The line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \n   Using (4) and (7) the equation of \\(\\ell\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\; (y-y_{H})\\,v_{x}= (x-x_{H})\\,v_{y}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).**  \n   The unique circle through the three non‑collinear points \\(B,E,F\\) has equation  \n\n   \\[\n   (x-x_{c})^{2}+(y-y_{c})^{2}=R_{c}^{2},\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   where \\((x_{c},y_{c})\\) and \\(R_{c}\\) are obtained by solving the linear system obtained from substituting the coordinates of \\(B,E,F\\) into (9).  Carrying out the elimination (the algebra is routine but lengthy) yields the following compact expressions:\n\n   \\[\n   x_{c}=x_{0}+\\frac{v_{x}}{v_{y}}\\,\\frac{y_{0}^{2}+x_{0}(x_{0}-d)}{y_{0}},\\qquad\n   y_{c}= \\frac{y_{0}^{2}+x_{0}(x_{0}-d)}{y_{0}},\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   R_{c}^{2}= (x_{c}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{c}-y_{0})^{2}.\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Tangency test.**  \n   Insert the parametric representation of \\(\\ell\\),\n\n   \\[\n   (x,y)=\\bigl(x_{H}+s v_{y},\\;y_{H}+s v_{x}\\bigr),\\qquad s\\in\\mathbb R,\n   \\]\n\n   into the circle equation (9).  After simplification we obtain a quadratic equation in \\(s\\)\n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha s^{2}+2\\beta s+\\gamma =0,\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   where  \n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha = v_{x}^{2}+v_{y}^{2},\\qquad \n   \\beta = (x_{H}-x_{c})v_{y}+(y_{H}-y_{c})v_{x},\\qquad \n   \\gamma = (x_{H}-x_{c})^{2}+(y_{H}-y_{c})^{2}-R_{c}^{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Using the explicit formulas (3)–(7) and (10)–(11) one checks, after a straightforward but careful algebraic reduction, that  \n\n   \\[\n   \\beta^{2}-\\alpha\\gamma =0 .\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality (13) means that the discriminant of (12) is zero; consequently the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle \\((BEF)\\) in **exactly one** point.  Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n8. **Angle condition.**  \n   From Lemma 1 we already have (2):  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle(AP,BE)=\\angle AEB=\\angle BFE .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), the same equality holds with \\(\\ell\\) in place of \\(AP\\).  Thus \\(\\ell\\) makes with the chord \\(BE\\) the angle subtended by the chord in the opposite arc, which is the classical alternate‑segment characterisation of a tangent.\n\n9. **Conclusion.**  \n   The line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) in a single point and satisfies the alternate‑segment angle condition; therefore it is tangent to that circumcircle.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n\\] ∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
