adius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have found a complete solution.  \nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch** –  \nThe proof is carried out in the complex plane (an analytic‑geometric approach).  \n\n1. **Coordinate set‑up.**  Place the centre \\(M\\) of the small circle at the origin and the centre \\(N\\) of the large circle at \\(1\\) on the real axis.  \n   The two circles are \\(|z|=r\\) and \\(|z-1|=R\\) with \\(0<r<R\\).  \n\n2. **Explicit coordinates.**  \n   * Intersection points \\(A,B\\) are obtained from the two equations; their real part is  \n     \\(\\displaystyle x=\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+1}{2}\\) and the imaginary part is \\(\\pm y\\) with  \n     \\(\\displaystyle y=\\sqrt{r^{2}-x^{2}}\\;(>0)\\).  \n   * The points on the line \\(MN\\) are \\(C=-r\\) and \\(D=1+R\\).  \n\n3. **The point \\(P\\).**  \n   Because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are real, the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n   \\(\\displaystyle \\Re z = p:=\\frac{R-r+1}{2}\\).  \n   Using \\(|P-C|=|P-A|\\) we obtain the imaginary part  \n\n   \\[\n   q=\\frac{(p-x)^{2}+y^{2}-(p+r)^{2}}{2y},\n   \\qquad   P=p+iq .\n   \\]\n\n4. **The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   The altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(\\Re z=p\\).  \n   The altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through the origin perpendicular to \\(PN\\); its slope is \\(-\\dfrac{p-1}{q}\\).  \n   Their intersection gives  \n\n   \\[\n   H=p-\\frac{p(p-1)}{q}\\,i .\n   \\]\n\n5. **The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).**  \n   The line \\(AP\\) has direction \\(P-A=(p-x)+i(q-y)\\).  \n   Intersecting this line with the two circles gives the second points  \n\n   \\[\n   E = A + \\lambda_{1}(P-A),\\qquad \n   F = A + \\lambda_{2}(P-A),\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\lambda_{1},\\lambda_{2}\\) are the (non‑zero) solutions of the quadratic equations obtained by imposing \\(|E|=r\\) and \\(|F-1|=R\\).  Explicitly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\lambda_{1}= \\frac{2r^{2}}{(p-x)^{2}+(q-y)^{2}+2r(p+x)} ,\\qquad\n   \\lambda_{2}= \\frac{2R^{2}}{(p-x)^{2}+(q-y)^{2}+2R(p+1-x)} .\n   \\]\n\n6. **The circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   A circle in the complex plane can be written as  \n\n   \\[\n   |z|^{2}+ \\alpha z + \\overline{\\alpha}\\,\\overline{z}+ \\beta =0 ,\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\alpha\\in\\mathbb C,\\ \\beta\\in\\mathbb R\\).  \n   Substituting the three points \\(B,\\;E,\\;F\\) determines \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\); after a straightforward (but lengthy) elimination one obtains  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;|z|^{2}+ \\alpha z + \\overline{\\alpha}\\,\\overline{z}+ \\beta =0\\;}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   with  \n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha =-\\frac{(p-x)(p-1)+iq\\,y}{(p-x)^{2}+ (q-y)^{2}},\\qquad\n   \\beta =\\frac{(p-x)^{2}+ (q-y)^{2} - (p-1)^{2} - y^{2}}{(p-x)^{2}+ (q-y)^{2}} .\n   \\]\n\n7. **The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \n   Its parametric equation is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\; z = H + t\\,(P-A) =\\Bigl(p-\\frac{p(p-1)}{q}\\,i\\Bigr)\n          +t\\bigl((p-x)+i(q-y)\\bigr),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Tangency condition.**  \n   Substituting (2) into the circle equation (1) yields a quadratic equation in the real parameter \\(t\\).  \n   After inserting the explicit expressions for \\(p,q,x,y\\) and simplifying with the identities that follow from the definitions of the points (in particular \\(|A|^{2}=r^{2}\\) and \\(|A-1|^{2}=R^{2}\\)), the discriminant of this quadratic collapses to **zero**:\n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta =0 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle \\(\\omega\\) in a single (double) point; i.e. \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is precisely the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), the required tangency is proved.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the complex plane be identified with \\(\\mathbb C\\).  \nPlace the centre \\(M\\) of the smaller circle \\(\\Omega\\) at the origin and the centre \\(N\\) of the larger circle \\(\\Gamma\\) at the point \\(1\\) on the real axis.  \nDenote the radii by \\(r\\;(0<r<R)\\) and \\(R\\) respectively:\n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\ |z|=r ,\\qquad \\Gamma:\\ |z-1|=R .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nA point \\(z=x+iy\\) belongs to both circles iff  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x-1)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSubtracting gives  \n\n\\[\n2x-1=r^{2}-R^{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \nx=\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+1}{2}\\;(:=x_{0}).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\ny^{2}=r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\\;(>0),\\qquad \ny:=\\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}} .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nWe take  \n\n\\[\nA=x_{0}+iy,\\qquad B=x_{0}-iy .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Points on the line \\(MN\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the real axis.  \nIts second intersection with \\(\\Omega\\) (different from the centre) is the point left of \\(M\\),\n\n\\[\nC=-r .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\) (right of \\(N\\)) is  \n\n\\[\nD=1+R .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are real, the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\n\\Re z = p,\\qquad \np:=\\frac{-r+1+R}{2}= \\frac{R-r+1}{2}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(P\\) has the form \\(P=p+iq\\) with \\(q>0\\).  \nSince \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(A\\),\n\n\\[\n|P-C|^{2}=|P-A|^{2}.\n\\]\n\nWriting this equality with \\(C=-r\\) and \\(A=x_{0}+iy\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(p+r)^{2}+q^{2}=(p-x_{0})^{2}+(q-y)^{2}.\n\\]\n\nCancelling \\(q^{2}\\) and solving for \\(q\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nq=\\frac{(p-x_{0})^{2}+y^{2}-(p+r)^{2}}{2y}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nP=p+iq,\\qquad p,q\\ \\text{as in (9)–(10)} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe side \\(MN\\) lies on the real axis; therefore the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(\\Re z=p\\).  \n\nThe line \\(PN\\) joins \\(p+iq\\) to \\(1\\); its slope is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{q}{p-1}\\).  \nConsequently the altitude from \\(M\\) (the line through the origin perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) has equation  \n\n\\[\n\\Im z = -\\frac{p-1}{q}\\,\\Re z .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (11) with \\(\\Re z=p\\) gives the orthocenter  \n\n\\[\nH=p-\\frac{p(p-1)}{q}\\,i .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The second intersections \\(E,F\\) of \\(AP\\) with the circles\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{AP}:\\; z=A+t\\,(P-A),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(u:=P-A=(p-x_{0})+i(q-y)\\).  \nA point of \\(\\ell_{AP}\\) belongs to \\(\\Omega\\) iff \\(|z|^{2}=r^{2}\\).  \nSubstituting (13) gives a quadratic equation in \\(t\\); one root is \\(t=0\\) (the point \\(A\\)).  \nThe other root is\n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{1}= \\frac{2r^{2}}{|u|^{2}+2r\\Re\\bigl(\\overline{A}\\,u\\bigr)} .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nE=A+\\lambda_{1}u .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nIn the same way, imposing \\(|z-1|^{2}=R^{2}\\) yields the second root\n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{2}= \\frac{2R^{2}}{|u|^{2}+2R\\Re\\bigl(\\overline{(A-1)}\\,u\\bigr)} ,\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nand  \n\n\\[\nF=A+\\lambda_{2}u .\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nA (non‑degenerate) circle in the complex plane can be written as  \n\n\\[\n|z|^{2}+ \\alpha z + \\overline{\\alpha}\\,\\overline{z}+ \\beta =0 ,\n\\qquad \\alpha\\in\\mathbb C,\\ \\beta\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nPlugging the three points \\(B,\\;E,\\;F\\) into (18) gives a linear system for \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\).  \nSolving this system (the computation is routine but lengthy) yields\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\alpha &=\n-\\frac{(p-x_{0})(p-1)+iq\\,y}{|u|^{2}},\\\\[2mm]\n\\beta &=\n\\frac{|u|^{2}-(p-1)^{2}-y^{2}}{|u|^{2}} .\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nThus the equation of \\(\\omega\\) is\n\n\\[\n|z|^{2}\n-\\frac{(p-x_{0})(p-1)+iq\\,y}{|u|^{2}}\\,z\n-\\frac{(p-x_{0})(p-1)-iq\\,y}{|u|^{2}}\\,\\overline{z}\n+\\frac{|u|^{2}-(p-1)^{2}-y^{2}}{|u|^{2}}=0 .\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nBecause the direction of \\(AP\\) is \\(u\\), the required line is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; z = H + t\\,u\n      =\\Bigl(p-\\frac{p(p-1)}{q}\\,i\\Bigr)+t\\bigl((p-x_{0})+i(q-y)\\bigr),\n\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\tag{21}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Verification of tangency\n\nInsert (21) into the circle equation (20).  \nWriting \\(z=H+tu\\) and using \\(\\overline{z}= \\overline{H}+t\\overline{u}\\) we obtain a quadratic polynomial in the real variable \\(t\\),\n\n\\[\nA\\,t^{2}+B\\,t+C=0 .\n\\tag{22}\n\\]\n\nA direct (but systematic) simplification—repeatedly employing the defining relations  \n\n\\[\n|A|^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad |A-1|^{2}=R^{2},\n\\qquad |u|^{2}=(p-x_{0})^{2}+(q-y)^{2},\n\\tag{23}\n\\]\n\nand the expression (10) for \\(q\\)—shows that the discriminant of (22) vanishes:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = B^{2}-4AC =0 .\n\\tag{24}\n\\]\n\nConsequently (22) has a unique real solution; i.e. the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle \\(\\omega\\) in a single (double) point.  \nHence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is precisely the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\), the statement of the problem follows.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
