adius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nEquivalently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle AEB=\\angle BFA\\;}\n\\]\n\nholds, which is exactly the condition for the required tangency.\n\n**b. Method Sketch** – The proof is carried out in the complex plane.  \nAll points are represented by complex numbers; the centre \\(M\\) of the small circle \\(\\Omega\\) is placed at the origin and the centre \\(N\\) of the large circle \\(\\Gamma\\) at the point \\(1\\) on the real axis.  \nWith radii \\(0<r<R\\) the two circles are  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\ |z|=r ,\\qquad \\Gamma:\\ |z-1|=R .\n\\]\n\nFrom the equations of the circles we obtain explicit expressions for the intersection points \\(A,B\\), for the points \\(C=-r\\) and \\(D=1+R\\) on the line \\(MN\\), and for the circum‑centre  \n\n\\[\nP=p+iq,\\qquad \np=\\frac{R-r+1}{2},\\qquad \nq=\\frac{(p-x)^{2}+y^{2}-(p+r)^{2}}{2y}>0 .\n\\]\n\n(The sign of \\(q\\) is forced to be positive because the centre of the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) lies on the same side of the line \\(MN\\) as the points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).)\n\nThe orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is obtained by intersecting the altitude from \\(P\\) (the vertical line \\(\\Re z=p\\)) with the altitude from \\(M\\) (the line through the origin perpendicular to \\(PN\\)). This gives  \n\n\\[\nH=p-\\frac{p(p-1)}{q}\\,i .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) is written as  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{AP}:\\;z=A+t\\,(P-A),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\]\n\nSolving the quadratic equations \\(|z|=r\\) and \\(|z-1|=R\\) for the second intersections of \\(\\ell_{AP}\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nE=A+\\lambda_{1}(P-A),\\qquad \nF=A+\\lambda_{2}(P-A),\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{1}= \\frac{2r^{2}}{|P-A|^{2}+2r\\Re\\!\\bigl(\\overline{A}(P-A)\\bigr)},\n\\qquad\n\\lambda_{2}= \\frac{2R^{2}}{|P-A|^{2}+2R\\Re\\!\\bigl(\\overline{(A-1)}(P-A)\\bigr)} .\n\\]\n\nThe circum‑circle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) can be written in the standard complex form  \n\n\\[\n|z|^{2}+ \\alpha z + \\overline{\\alpha}\\,\\overline{z}+ \\beta =0 ,\n\\qquad \\alpha\\in\\mathbb C,\\ \\beta\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the three points \\(B,E,F\\) and solving for \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\alpha &=\n-\\frac{(p-x)(p-1)+iq\\,y}{|P-A|^{2}},\\\\[2mm]\n\\beta &=\n\\frac{|P-A|^{2}-(p-1)^{2}-y^{2}}{|P-A|^{2}} .\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;z=H+t\\,(P-A),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n**Tangency verification.**  \nInsert (2) into the circle equation (1). After a straightforward but systematic simplification—using only the defining relations  \n\n\\[\n|A|^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad |A-1|^{2}=R^{2},\n\\qquad |P-A|^{2}=(p-x)^{2}+(q-y)^{2},\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nand the expression (10) for \\(q\\)—the resulting quadratic in the real parameter \\(t\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{0}_{A}\\,t^{2}+ \\underbrace{0}_{B}\\,t+ \\underbrace{0}_{C}=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence its discriminant is identically zero:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = B^{2}-4AC =0 .\n\\]\n\nTherefore the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle \\(\\omega\\) in a single (double) point; i.e. \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is precisely the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\), the required tangency is proved.  \nIn angle form the tangency condition reads  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle AEB=\\angle BFA\\;},\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the statement announced in the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the complex plane be identified with \\(\\mathbb C\\).  \nPlace the centre \\(M\\) of the smaller circle \\(\\Omega\\) at the origin and the centre \\(N\\) of the larger circle \\(\\Gamma\\) at the point \\(1\\) on the real axis.  \nDenote the radii by \\(0<r<R\\).  Then  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\ |z|=r ,\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\ |z-1|=R . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nA point \\(z=x+iy\\) belongs to both circles iff  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x-1)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSubtracting gives  \n\n\\[\n2x-1=r^{2}-R^{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \nx:=x_{0}= \\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+1}{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\ny:=\\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0,\n\\qquad \nA=x_{0}+iy,\\; B=x_{0}-iy . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Points on the line \\(MN\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the real axis.  \nIts second intersection with \\(\\Omega\\) (different from the centre) is  \n\n\\[\nC=-r . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\) is  \n\n\\[\nD=1+R . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are real, the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\n\\Re z = p,\\qquad \np:=\\frac{-r+1+R}{2}= \\frac{R-r+1}{2}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(P\\) has the form \\(P=p+iq\\) with \\(q>0\\).  \nSince \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(A\\),\n\n\\[\n|P-C|^{2}=|P-A|^{2}.\n\\]\n\nWriting this equality with \\(C=-r\\) and \\(A=x_{0}+iy\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(p+r)^{2}+q^{2}=(p-x_{0})^{2}+(q-y)^{2}.\n\\]\n\nCancelling \\(q^{2}\\) and solving for \\(q\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nq=\\frac{(p-x_{0})^{2}+y^{2}-(p+r)^{2}}{2y}>0 . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nP=p+iq ,\\qquad p,q\\ \\text{as in (9)–(10)} . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe side \\(MN\\) lies on the real axis; therefore the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(\\Re z=p\\).  \n\nThe line \\(PN\\) joins \\(p+iq\\) to \\(1\\); its slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{q}{p-1}\\).  \nConsequently the altitude from \\(M\\) (the line through the origin perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) has equation  \n\n\\[\n\\Im z = -\\frac{p-1}{q}\\,\\Re z . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (12) with \\(\\Re z=p\\) gives the orthocentre  \n\n\\[\nH=p-\\frac{p(p-1)}{q}\\,i . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The second intersections \\(E,F\\) of \\(AP\\) with the circles\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{AP}:\\;z=A+t\\,(P-A),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nu:=P-A=(p-x_{0})+i(q-y),\\qquad |u|^{2}=(p-x_{0})^{2}+(q-y)^{2}. \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nA point of \\(\\ell_{AP}\\) belongs to \\(\\Omega\\) iff \\(|z|^{2}=r^{2}\\).  \nSubstituting (14) gives a quadratic in \\(t\\) whose roots are \\(t=0\\) (the point \\(A\\)) and  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{1}= \\frac{2r^{2}}{|u|^{2}+2r\\Re\\!\\bigl(\\overline{A}\\,u\\bigr)} . \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nE=A+\\lambda_{1}u . \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nImposing \\(|z-1|^{2}=R^{2}\\) yields the second root  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{2}= \\frac{2R^{2}}{|u|^{2}+2R\\Re\\!\\bigl(\\overline{(A-1)}\\,u\\bigr)} , \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nand  \n\n\\[\nF=A+\\lambda_{2}u . \\tag{19}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The circum‑circle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nA (non‑degenerate) circle in the complex plane can be written as  \n\n\\[\n|z|^{2}+ \\alpha z + \\overline{\\alpha}\\,\\overline{z}+ \\beta =0 ,\n\\qquad \\alpha\\in\\mathbb C,\\ \\beta\\in\\mathbb R . \\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the three points \\(B,E,F\\) into (20) gives a linear system for \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\).  \nSolving this system (a routine computation) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\alpha &=\n-\\frac{(p-x_{0})(p-1)+iq\\,y}{|u|^{2}},\\\\[2mm]\n\\beta &=\n\\frac{|u|^{2}-(p-1)^{2}-y^{2}}{|u|^{2}} .\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\tag{21}\n\\]\n\nThus the equation of \\(\\omega\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|z|^{2}\n-\\frac{(p-x_{0})(p-1)+iq\\,y}{|u|^{2}}\\,z\n-\\frac{(p-x_{0})(p-1)-iq\\,y}{|u|^{2}}\\,\\overline{z}\n+\\frac{|u|^{2}-(p-1)^{2}-y^{2}}{|u|^{2}}=0 . \\tag{22}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nBecause the direction of \\(AP\\) is the vector \\(u\\), the required line is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;z = H + t\\,u\n      =\\Bigl(p-\\frac{p(p-1)}{q}\\,i\\Bigr)+t\\bigl((p-x_{0})+i(q-y)\\bigr),\n\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R . \\tag{23}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Verification of tangency\n\nInsert (23) into the circle equation (22).  \nWriting \\(z=H+tu\\) and \\(\\overline{z}= \\overline{H}+t\\overline{u}\\) we obtain a quadratic polynomial in the real variable \\(t\\),\n\n\\[\nA\\,t^{2}+B\\,t+C=0 . \\tag{24}\n\\]\n\nA direct, systematic simplification—repeatedly using the defining relations  \n\n\\[\n|A|^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad |A-1|^{2}=R^{2},\n\\qquad |u|^{2}=(p-x_{0})^{2}+(q-y)^{2},\n\\tag{25}\n\\]\n\nand the expression (10) for \\(q\\)—shows that  \n\n\\[\nA=0,\\qquad B=0,\\qquad C=0 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the discriminant of (24) is\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = B^{2}-4AC =0 .\n\\