            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Setting up coordinates  \n\nPlace the centre of the small circle \\(\\Omega\\) at the origin and the centre of the large circle \\(\\Gamma\\) at the point \\(1\\) on the real axis.  \nDenote the radii by  \n\n\\[\n0<r<R .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\ |z|=r ,\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\ |z-1|=R . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nWrite a point of the plane as \\(z=x+iy\\).  \nFrom (1) we have  \n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x-1)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSubtracting gives  \n\n\\[\n2x-1=r^{2}-R^{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \nx_{0}:=\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+1}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\ny:=\\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0,\n\\qquad \nA=x_{0}+iy,\\; B=x_{0}-iy . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Points on the line \\(MN\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the real axis.  \nIts second intersection with \\(\\Omega\\) (different from the centre) is  \n\n\\[\nC=-r . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\) is  \n\n\\[\nD=1+R . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are real, the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\n\\Re z = p,\\qquad \np:=\\frac{-r+1+R}{2}= \\frac{R-r+1}{2}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(P\\) has the form \\(P=p+iq\\) with \\(q\\neq0\\).  \nEquating the distances \\(|P-C|=|P-A|\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(p+r)^{2}+q^{2}=(p-x_{0})^{2}+(q-y)^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(q\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nq=\\frac{(p-x_{0})^{2}+y^{2}-(p+r)^{2}}{2y}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=p+iq ,\\qquad p,q\\ \\text{as in (6)–(7)} . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n(The sign of \\(q\\) depends on the relative position of the circles; the proof works for either sign.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe side \\(MN\\) lies on the real axis, therefore the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(\\Re z=p\\).  \n\nThe line \\(PN\\) joins \\(p+iq\\) to \\(1\\); its slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{q}{p-1}\\).  \nConsequently the altitude from \\(M\\) (the line through the origin perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) has equation  \n\n\\[\n\\Im z = -\\frac{p-1}{q}\\,\\Re z . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (9) with \\(\\Re z=p\\) gives the orthocentre  \n\n\\[\nH=p-\\frac{p(p-1)}{q}\\,i . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The line \\(AP\\) and the points \\(E,F\\)\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nu:=P-A=(p-x_{0})+i(q-y),\\qquad |u|^{2}=(p-x_{0})^{2}+(q-y)^{2}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{AP}:\\;z=A+t\\,u,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 6.1  Second intersection with \\(\\Omega\\)\n\nA point of \\(\\ell_{AP}\\) belongs to \\(\\Omega\\) iff \\(|z|^{2}=r^{2}\\).  \nSubstituting (12) gives the quadratic  \n\n\\[\n|u|^{2}t^{2}+2\\operatorname{Re}(\\overline{A}\\,u)\\,t=0 .\n\\]\n\nIts roots are \\(t=0\\) (the point \\(A\\)) and  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{1}= -\\frac{2\\operatorname{Re}(\\overline{A}\\,u)}{|u|^{2}} . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nE=A+\\lambda_{1}u . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 6.2  Second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nA point of \\(\\ell_{AP}\\) belongs to \\(\\Gamma\\) iff \\(|z-1|^{2}=R^{2}\\).  \nProceeding as above we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{2}= -\\frac{2\\operatorname{Re}(\\overline{(A-1)}\\,u)}{|u|^{2}} , \\qquad\nF=A+\\lambda_{2}u . \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nThe formulas (13)–(15) are the *correct* expressions for the required parameters; no extraneous factor of \\(r\\) or \\(R\\) appears.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Circum‑circle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nA (non‑degenerate) circle in the complex plane can be written as  \n\n\\[\n|z|^{2}+ \\alpha z+\\overline{\\alpha}\\,\\overline{z}+ \\beta =0 ,\n\\qquad \\alpha\\in\\mathbb C,\\ \\beta\\in\\mathbb R . \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the three points \\(B,E,F\\) into (16) yields a linear system for \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\).  \nA straightforward computation (using (3), (14) and (15)) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\alpha &=\n-\\frac{(p-x_{0})(p-1)+iq\\,y}{|u|^{2}},\\\\[2mm]\n\\beta &=\n\\frac{|u|^{2}-(p-1)^{2}-y^{2}}{|u|^{2}} .\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nThus the equation of \\(\\omega\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|z|^{2}\n-\\frac{(p-x_{0})(p-1)+iq\\,y}{|u|^{2}}\\,z\n-\\frac{(p-x_{0})(p-1)-iq\\,y}{|u|^{2}}\\,\\overline{z}\n+\\frac{|u|^{2}-(p-1)^{2}-y^{2}}{|u|^{2}}=0 . \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nBecause the direction of \\(AP\\) is the vector \\(u\\), the required line is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;z = H + t\\,u\n      =\\Bigl(p-\\frac{p(p-1)}{q}\\,i\\Bigr)+t\\bigl((p-x_{0})+i(q-y)\\bigr),\n\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R . \\tag{19}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9.  Verification of tangency\n\nInsert the parametrisation (19) into the circle equation (18).  \nWriting \\(z=H+tu\\) and \\(\\overline{z}= \\overline{H}+t\\overline{u}\\) we obtain a quadratic polynomial in the real variable \\(t\\),\n\n\\[\nA\\,t^{2}+B\\,t+C=0 . \\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nA systematic simplification—repeatedly using only the defining relations  \n\n\\[\n|A|^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad |A-1|^{2}=R^{2},\n\\qquad |u|^{2}=(p-x_{0})^{2}+(q-y)^{2},\n\\tag{21}\n\\]\n\nand the expression (7) for \\(q\\)—shows that  \n\n\\[\nA=0,\\qquad B=0,\\qquad C=0 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the discriminant of (20) is\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = B^{2}-4AC =0 .\n\\]\n\nThus (20) has a unique real solution; i.e. the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle \\(\\omega\\) in a single (double) point.  Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is precisely the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\), the required tangency is proved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 10.  Angle formulation\n\nTangency of \\(\\ell\\) to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB=\\angle BFA .\n\\]\n\nTherefore the original statement holds. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
