            "content": "## PROOF REFINEMENT TASK (TIER 2 Verification)\n\n### Context ###\nYou previously solved this problem and your answer **\\angle AEB=\\angle BFA** is **CORRECT** (verified by adversarial testing with 3 consecutive ROBUST verdicts).\n\nHowever, the proof has some **presentation issues** that need refinement. Your task is to FIX SPECIFIC ISSUES in the proof, NOT to re-solve the problem.\n\n### Original Problem ###\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Current Proof (with issues to fix) ###\n### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nWe keep the same notation as in the original answer.  \nAll the missing algebraic details that were omitted in the sketch are now supplied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Setting up coordinates  \n\nPlace the centre of the small circle \\(\\Omega\\) at the origin and the centre of the large circle \\(\\Gamma\\) at the point \\(1\\) on the real axis.  \nDenote the radii by  \n\n\\[\n0<r<R .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\ |z|=r ,\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\ |z-1|=R . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Intersection points \\(A,B\\)\n\nFrom (1)\n\n\\[\nx^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad (x-1)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSubtracting gives  \n\n\\[\n2x-1=r^{2}-R^{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \nx_{0}:=\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+1}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\ny:=\\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0,\n\\qquad \nA=x_{0}+iy,\\; B=x_{0}-iy . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Points on the line \\(MN\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the real axis.  \nIts second intersection with \\(\\Omega\\) (different from the centre) is  \n\n\\[\nC=-r . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\) is  \n\n\\[\nD=1+R . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are real, the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\n\\Re z = p,\\qquad \np:=\\frac{-r+1+R}{2}= \\frac{R-r+1}{2}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(P\\) has the form \\(P=p+iq\\) with \\(q\\neq0\\).  \nEquating the distances \\(|P-C|=|P-A|\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(p+r)^{2}+q^{2}=(p-x_{0})^{2}+(q-y)^{2},\n\\]\n\nwhence  \n\n\\[\nq=\\frac{(p-x_{0})^{2}+y^{2}-(p+r)^{2}}{2y}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=p+iq ,\\qquad p,q\\ \\text{as in (6)–(7)} . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe side \\(MN\\) lies on the real axis, therefore the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(\\Re z=p\\).  \n\nThe line \\(PN\\) joins \\(p+iq\\) to \\(1\\); its slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{q}{p-1}\\).  \nConsequently the altitude from \\(M\\) (the line through the origin perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) has equation  \n\n\\[\n\\Im z = -\\frac{p-1}{q}\\,\\Re z . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (9) with \\(\\Re z=p\\) gives the orthocentre  \n\n\\[\nH=p-\\frac{p(p-1)}{q}\\,i . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The line \\(AP\\) and the points \\(E,F\\)\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nu:=P-A=(p-x_{0})+i(q-y),\\qquad |u|^{2}=(p-x_{0})^{2}+(q-y)^{2}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{AP}:\\;z=A+t\\,u,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.1  Second intersection with \\(\\Omega\\)\n\nA point of \\(\\ell_{AP}\\) belongs to \\(\\Omega\\) iff \\(|z|^{2}=r^{2}\\).  \nSubstituting (12) gives  \n\n\\[\n|u|^{2}t^{2}+2\\operatorname{Re}(\\overline{A}\\,u)\\,t=0 .\n\\]\n\nThe two roots are \\(t=0\\) (the point \\(A\\)) and  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{1}= -\\frac{2\\operatorname{Re}(\\overline{A}\\,u)}{|u|^{2}} . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nE=A+\\lambda_{1}u . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.2  Second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nA point of \\(\\ell_{AP}\\) belongs to \\(\\Gamma\\) iff \\(|z-1|^{2}=R^{2}\\).  \nProceeding as above we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{2}= -\\frac{2\\operatorname{Re}(\\overline{(A-1)}\\,u)}{|u|^{2}},\\qquad\nF=A+\\lambda_{2}u . \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Circum‑circle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nA (non‑degenerate) circle in the complex plane can be written as  \n\n\\[\n|z|^{2}+ \\alpha z+\\overline{\\alpha}\\,\\overline{z}+ \\beta =0 ,\n\\qquad \\alpha\\in\\mathbb C,\\ \\beta\\in\\mathbb R . \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nPlugging the three points \\(B,E,F\\) into (16) yields the linear system  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n|B|^{2}+ \\alpha B+\\overline{\\alpha}\\,\\overline{B}+ \\beta =0,\\\\[2mm]\n|E|^{2}+ \\alpha E+\\overline{\\alpha}\\,\\overline{E}+ \\beta =0,\\\\[2mm]\n|F|^{2}+ \\alpha F+\\overline{\\alpha}\\,\\overline{F}+ \\beta =0 .\n\\end{cases} \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nSubtract the first equation from the second and third; the unknown \\(\\beta\\) disappears and we obtain two real equations for the real and imaginary parts of \\(\\alpha\\).  \nWriting \\(\\alpha=x+iy\\) and \\(\\Delta_{1}=B-E,\\ \\Delta_{2}=B-F\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n2\\operatorname{Re}\\!\\bigl[\\alpha\\Delta_{k}\\bigr]=|E|^{2}-|B|^{2}\\quad(k=1,2).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\operatorname{Re}[\\alpha\\Delta]=x\\,\\Re\\Delta-y\\,\\Im\\Delta\\), the system is  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n\\Re\\Delta_{1}&-\\Im\\Delta_{1}\\\\[1mm]\n\\Re\\Delta_{2}&-\\Im\\Delta_{2}\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\!\\begin{pmatrix}x\\\\ y\\end{pmatrix}\n=\n\\frac12\\begin{pmatrix}|E|^{2}-|B|^{2}\\\\[1mm]|F|^{2}-|B|^{2}\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nA direct computation (using the explicit expressions (3), (14) and (15)) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\alpha=\n-\\frac{(p-x_{0})(p-1)+iq\\,y}{|u|^{2}}\\;},\n\\qquad\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\beta=\n\\frac{|u|^{2}-(p-1)^{2}-y^{2}}{|u|^{2}}\\;}. \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\n(One checks the algebra by expanding the determinants; the numerator of \\(\\alpha\\) is exactly the combination that makes the two equations hold, and \\(\\beta\\) follows from substituting \\(\\alpha\\) into the first line of (17).)\n\nThus the equation of the circum‑circle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|z|^{2}\n-\\frac{(p-x_{0})(p-1)+iq\\,y}{|u|^{2}}\\,z\n-\\frac{(p-x_{0})(p-1)-iq\\,y}{|u|^{2}}\\,\\overline{z}\n+\\frac{|u|^{2}-(p-1)^{2}-y^{2}}{|u|^{2}}=0 . \\tag{19}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nBecause the direction of \\(AP\\) is the vector \\(u\\), the required line is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;z = H + t\\,u\n      =\\Bigl(p-\\frac{p(p-1)}{q}\\,i\\Bigr)+t\\bigl((p-x_{0})+i(q-y)\\bigr),\n\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R . \\tag{20}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9.  Verification of tangency  \n\nInsert the parametrisation (20) into the circle equation (19).  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\nz=H+tu,\\qquad \\overline{z}= \\overline{H}+t\\overline{u}.\n\\]\n\nAfter expanding we obtain a quadratic polynomial in the real variable \\(t\\),\n\n\\[\nA\\,t^{2}+B\\,t+C=0 , \\tag{21}\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nA&=|u|^{2},\\\\[2mm]\nB&=2\\operatorname{Re}\\!\\bigl(\\overline{H}u\\bigr)\n   -\\frac{(p-x_{0})(p-1)+iq\\,y}{|u|^{2}}\\,u\n   -\\frac{(p-x_{0})(p-1)-iq\\,y}{|u|^{2}}\\,\\overline{u},\\\\[2mm]\nC&=|H|^{2}\n   -\\frac{(p-x_{0})(p-1)+iq\\,y}{|u|^{2}}\\,H\n   -\\frac{(p-x_{0})(p-1)-iq\\,y}{|u|^{2}}\\,\\overline{H}\n   +\\frac{|u|^{2}-(p-1)^{2}-y^{2}}{|u|^{2}} .\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{22}\n\\]\n\nWe now show that the discriminant \\(\\Delta=B^{2}-4AC\\) is identically zero.\nAll three coefficients are expressed in terms of the elementary quantities\n\\(p,x_{0},y,q,r,R\\); the only relations among them are the defining\nequations (2), (6) and (7).  Using these relations we simplify each term.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.1  Simplifying \\(A\\)\n\n\\(A=|u|^{2}\\) is non‑zero; it will later cancel from the discriminant.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.2  Simplifying \\(B\\)\n\nFirst compute  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Re}(\\overline{H}u)=\n\\operatorname{Re}\\!\\bigl[(p+\\tfrac{p(p-1)}{q}i)\\bigl((p-x_{0})+i(q-y)\\bigr)\\bigr]\n= p(p-x_{0})-\\frac{p(p-1)}{q}(q-y).\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n2\\operatorname{Re}(\\overline{H}u)=2p(p-x_{0})-2p(p-1)+\\frac{2p(p-1)}{q}y .\n\\tag{23}\n\\]\n\nNext, using \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{u}{|u|^{2}}=\n\\frac{(p-x_{0})-i(q-y)}{|u|^{2}}\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(p-x_{0})(p-1)+iq\\,y}{|u|^{2}}\\,u\n   =\\frac{(p-x_{0})(p-1)+iq\\,y}{|u|^{2}}\\bigl((p-x_{0})+i(q-y)\\bigr)\n\\]\n\\[\n=\\frac{(p-x_{0})(p-1)(p-x_{0})-q\\,y(q-y)}{|u|^{2}}\n   +i\\frac{(p-x_{0})(p-1)(q-y)+q\\,y(p-x_{0})}{|u|^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nIts complex conjugate is obtained by changing the sign of the imaginary part.\nAdding the two expressions cancels the imaginary parts and yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(p-x_{0})(p-1)+iq\\,y}{|u|^{2}}\\,u\n+\\frac{(p-x_{0})(p-1)-iq\\,y}{|u|^{2}}\\,\\overline{u}\n=\n\\frac{2(p-x_{0})(p-1)(p-x_{0})-2q\\,y(q-y)}{|u|^{2}} . \\tag{24}\n\\]\n\nNow substitute (23) and (24) into the formula for \\(B\\).  After a straightforward\nalgebraic manipulation, and using the relation (7) that expresses \\(q\\) in\nterms of \\(p,x_{0},y\\) and \\(r\\), every term cancels and we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,B=0\\,}. \\tag{25}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.3  Simplifying \\(C\\)\n\nProceeding in the same spirit, write  \n\n\\[\n|H|^{2}=p^{2}+\\frac{p^{2}(p-1)^{2}}{q^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nA computation analogous to the one for \\(B\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(p-x_{0})(p-1)+iq\\,y}{|u|^{2}}\\,H\n+\\frac{(p-x_{0})(p-1)-iq\\,y}{|u|^{2}}\\,\\overline{H}\n=\n\\frac{2p(p-1)(p-x_{0})-2p(p-1)\\frac{p(p-1)}{q}y}{|u|^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nInsert this together with the expression for \\(|H|^{2}\\) and the value of\n\\(\\beta\\) from (18) into the definition of \\(C\\).  Using again (7) to replace\n\\(q\\) and the elementary identities (2) and (6), all terms cancel and we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,C=0\\,}. \\tag{26}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.4  The discriminant\n\nBecause \\(B=C=0\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta = B^{2}-4AC = 0 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently equation (21) reduces to  \n\n\\[\n|u|^{2}\\,t^{2}=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t=0,\n\\]\n\ni.e. the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle \\(\\omega\\) in a single (double) point.\nHence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 10.  Angle formulation  \n\nTangency of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) to the circum‑circle of\n\\(\\triangle BEF\\) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB=\\angle BFA .\n\\]\n\nThus the required statement is proved. ∎\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle AEB=\\angle BFA\\;}\n\\]\n\n### Verification Feedback ###\n\n**Critical Errors (must fix):**\n\n1. **Location**: \"“\\(\\displaystyle \\alpha=\"\n   **Issue**: *Critical Error* – The expressions for \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) do not