r+1+R}{2}= \\frac{R-r+1}{2}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(P\\) has the form \\(P=p+iq\\) with \\(q\\neq0\\).  \nEquating the distances \\(|P-C|=|P-A|\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(p+r)^{2}+q^{2}=(p-x_{0})^{2}+(q-y)^{2},\n\\]\n\nwhence  \n\n\\[\nq=\\frac{(p-x_{0})^{2}+y^{2}-(p+r)^{2}}{2y}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=p+iq ,\\qquad p,q\\ \\text{as in (6)–(7)} . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe side \\(MN\\) lies on the real axis, therefore the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(\\Re z=p\\).  \n\nThe line \\(PN\\) joins \\(p+iq\\) to \\(1\\); its slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{q}{p-1}\\).  \nConsequently the altitude from \\(M\\) (the line through the origin perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) has equation  \n\n\\[\n\\Im z = -\\frac{p-1}{q}\\,\\Re z . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (9) with \\(\\Re z=p\\) gives the orthocentre  \n\n\\[\nH=p-\\frac{p(p-1)}{q}\\,i . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The line \\(AP\\) and the points \\(E,F\\)\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nu:=P-A=(p-x_{0})+i(q-y),\\qquad |u|^{2}=(p-x_{0})^{2}+(q-y)^{2}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{AP}:\\;z=A+t\\,u,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.1  Second intersection with \\(\\Omega\\)\n\nA point of \\(\\ell_{AP}\\) belongs to \\(\\Omega\\) iff \\(|z|^{2}=r^{2}\\).  \nSubstituting (12) gives  \n\n\\[\n|u|^{2}t^{2}+2\\operatorname{Re}(\\overline{A}\\,u)\\,t+|A|^{2}-r^{2}=0 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(A\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) we have \\(|A|^{2}=r^{2}\\); the constant term vanishes and the\nquadratic factorises as  \n\n\\[\nt\\bigl(|u|^{2}t+2\\operatorname{Re}(\\overline{A}\\,u)\\bigr)=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence the two roots are \\(t=0\\) (the point \\(A\\)) and  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\lambda_{1}= -\\frac{2\\operatorname{Re}(\\overline{A}\\,u)}{|u|^{2}} } . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nE=A+\\lambda_{1}u . \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.2  Second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\)\n\nA point of \\(\\ell_{AP}\\) belongs to \\(\\Gamma\\) iff \\(|z-1|^{2}=R^{2}\\).  \nProceeding as above we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|u|^{2}t^{2}+2\\operatorname{Re}\\!\\bigl(\\overline{(A-1)}\\,u\\bigr)\\,t+|A-1|^{2}-R^{2}=0 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(A\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\) we have \\(|A-1|^{2}=R^{2}\\); the constant term again vanishes and  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\lambda_{2}= -\\frac{2\\operatorname{Re}\\!\\bigl(\\overline{(A-1)}\\,u\\bigr)}{|u|^{2}} } . \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nF=A+\\lambda_{2}u . \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Circum‑circle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nA (non‑degenerate) circle in the complex plane can be written as  \n\n\\[\n|z|^{2}+ \\alpha z+\\overline{\\alpha}\\,\\overline{z}+ \\beta =0 ,\n\\qquad \\alpha\\in\\mathbb C,\\ \\beta\\in\\mathbb R . \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nPlugging the three points \\(B,E,F\\) into (17) yields the linear system  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n|B|^{2}+ \\alpha B+\\overline{\\alpha}\\,\\overline{B}+ \\beta =0,\\\\[2mm]\n|E|^{2}+ \\alpha E+\\overline{\\alpha}\\,\\overline{E}+ \\beta =0,\\\\[2mm]\n|F|^{2}+ \\alpha F+\\overline{\\alpha}\\,\\overline{F}+ \\beta =0 .\n\\end{cases} \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nSubtract the first equation from the second and third; the unknown \\(\\beta\\) disappears and we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\bigl(|E|^{2}-|B|^{2}\\bigr)+\\alpha (E-B)+\\overline{\\alpha}\\,(\\overline{E}-\\overline{B})=0,\\\\\n&\\bigl(|F|^{2}-|B|^{2}\\bigr)+\\alpha (F-B)+\\overline{\\alpha}\\,(\\overline{F}-\\overline{B})=0 .\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(\\Delta_{E}=E-B,\\ \\Delta_{F}=F-B\\) and \\(\\alpha=x+iy\\) with \\(x,y\\in\\mathbb R\\).\nSince \\(\\alpha\\Delta+\\overline{\\alpha}\\,\\overline{\\Delta}=2\\operatorname{Re}(\\alpha\\Delta)\\), the two equations become  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\operatorname{Re}(\\alpha\\Delta_{E})&=|B|^{2}-|E|^{2},\\\\\n2\\operatorname{Re}(\\alpha\\Delta_{F})&=|B|^{2}-|F|^{2}.\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(B\\) and \\(E\\) both lie on \\(\\Omega\\) we have \\(|B|^{2}=|E|^{2}=r^{2}\\); the first equation reduces to  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Re}(\\alpha\\Delta_{E})=0 . \\tag{21}\n\\]\n\nEquation (21) says that the complex numbers \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\Delta_{E}\\) are orthogonal in the Euclidean sense, i.e. their scalar product is zero.  Any complex number orthogonal to a given non‑zero \\(\\Delta_{E}\\) can be written as a real multiple of \\(i\\overline{\\Delta_{E}}\\) (rotation by \\(90^{\\circ}\\)).  Hence there exists a real number \\(t\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\alpha=i\\,t\\,\\overline{\\Delta_{E}}}. \\tag{22}\n\\]\n\nInsert (22) into the second equation of (20).  Using \\(\\operatorname{Re}(i\\,w)=-\\operatorname{Im}(w)\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n-\\,t\\,\\operatorname{Im}\\!\\bigl(\\overline{\\Delta_{E}}\\Delta_{F}\\bigr)=\\frac{|B|^{2}-|F|^{2}}{2}\n   =\\frac{r^{2}-|F|^{2}}{2}. \\tag{23}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nt=-\\frac{r^{2}-|F|^{2}}{2\\,\\operatorname{Im}\\!\\bigl(\\overline{\\Delta_{E}}\\Delta_{F}\\bigr)}\n   =\\frac{|F|^{2}-r^{2}}{2\\,\\operatorname{Im}\\!\\bigl(\\overline{\\Delta_{E}}\\Delta_{F}\\bigr)} .\n\\]\n\nCombining this value of \\(t\\) with (22) gives the **exact** expression for \\(\\alpha\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\alpha=\\frac{|F|^{2}-r^{2}}{2\\,\\operatorname{Im}\\!\\bigl(\\overline{\\Delta_{E}}\\Delta_{F}\\bigr)}\\;\n      i\\,\\overline{\\Delta_{E}} } . \\tag{24}\n\\]\n\nFinally, \\(\\beta\\) follows from the first line of (18):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\beta=-|B|^{2}-\\bigl(\\alpha B+\\overline{\\alpha}\\,\\overline{B}\\bigr)\n      =-r^{2}-2\\operatorname{Re}(\\alpha B)} . \\tag{25}\n\\]\n\nThus the equation of the circum‑circle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|z|^{2}+ \\alpha z+\\overline{\\alpha}\\,\\overline{z}+ \\beta =0 ,\n\\qquad\\text{with }\\alpha,\\beta\\text{ given by (24)–(25)} . \\tag{26}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nBecause the direction of \\(AP\\) is the vector \\(u\\), the required line is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;z = H + t\\,u\n      =\\Bigl(p-\\frac{p(p-1)}{q}\\,i\\Bigr)+t\\bigl((p-x_{0})+i(q-y)\\bigr),\n\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R . \\tag{27}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9.  Verification of tangency  \n\nInsert the parametrisation (27) into the circle equation (26).  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\nz=H+tu,\\qquad \\overline{z}= \\overline{H}+t\\overline{u}.\n\\]\n\nAfter expanding we obtain a quadratic polynomial in the real variable \\(t\\),\n\n\\[\nA\\,t^{2}+B\\,t+C=0 , \\tag{28}\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nA&=|u|^{2},\\\\[2mm]\nB&=2\\operatorname{Re}\\!\\bigl(\\overline{H}u\\bigr)\n   +\\alpha u+\\overline{\\alpha}\\,\\overline{u}\n   =2\\operatorname{Re}\\!\\bigl((\\overline{H}+\\alpha)u\\bigr),\\\\[2mm]\nC&=|H|^{2}+\\alpha H+\\overline{\\alpha}\\,\\overline{H}+\\beta\n   =|H|^{2}+2\\operatorname{Re}(\\alpha H)+\\beta .\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{29}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) iff the discriminant of (28) vanishes, i.e.  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta:=B^{2}-4AC=0 . \\tag{30}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.1  Useful auxiliary identities  \n\nFrom (2), (6) and (7) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{0}&=\\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+1}{2},\\qquad \np   =\\frac{R-r+1}{2},\\\\[1mm]\nq   &=\\frac{(p-x_{0})^{2}+y^{2}-(p+r)^{2}}{2y}\n     =-\\frac{p\\,(x_{0}+r)}{y}\\qquad\\text{(after expanding the numerator and using }y^{2}=r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\\text{)}. \n\\end{aligned} \\tag{31}\n\\]\n\nMoreover, using the definitions of \\(\\lambda_{1},\\lambda_{2}\\) from (13)–(15) and the expression \\(u=P-A\\) we compute  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lambda_{1}&=-\\frac{2\\bigl(x_{0}(p-x_{0})+y(q-y)\\bigr)}{|u|^{2}},\\\\[1mm]\n\\lambda_{2}&=-\\frac{2\\bigl((x_{0}-1)(p-x_{0})+y(q-y)\\bigr)}{|u|^{2}},\\\\[1mm]\n\\lambda_{1}-\\lambda_{2}&=-\\frac{2(p-x_{0})}{|u|^{2}} .\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{32}\n\\]\n\nA short computation using (31) gives  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}(p-x_{0})+y(q-y)=-\\,r\\,(p+r). \\tag{33}\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{0}(p-x_{0})+y(q-y)\n&=x_{0}p-x_{0}^{2}+yq-y^{2}\\\\\n&=x_{0}p-x_{0}^{2}+y\\!\\Bigl(-\\frac{p(x_{0}+r)}{y}\\Bigr)-\\bigl(r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=x_{0}p-x_{0}^{2}-p(x_{0}+r)-r^{2}+x_{0}^{2}\\\\\n&= -p r -r^{2}= -r(p+r).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nFrom the definitions \\(E=A+\\lambda_{1}u\\) and \\(F=A+\\lambda_{2}u\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_{E}=E-B=\\lambda_{1}u+(A-B),\\qquad \n\\Delta_{F}=F-B=\\lambda_{2}u+(A-B).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(A-B=2iy\\) is purely imaginary, a direct expansion yields  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Im}\\!\\bigl(\\overline{\\Delta_{E}}\\Delta_{F}\\bigr)\n   =\\operatorname{Im}\\!\\bigl(\\overline{u}u\\,\\lambda_{1}\\lambda_{2}\n        +\\overline{u}(A-B)\\lambda_{2}\n        +(A-B)^{\\!*}\\!u\\lambda_{1}\n        +\\overline{(A-B)}(A-B)\\bigr)\n   =-\\frac{4y\\,(p-x_{0})^{2}}{|u|^{2}} . \\tag{34}\n\\]\n\n(The mixed terms cancel because \\(\\overline{u}(A-B)\\) and \\((A-B)^{\\!*}u\\) are complex conjugates; the remaining term \\(\\overline{(A-B)}(A-B)=4y^{2}\\) is real and does not affect the imaginary part.  Substituting the value of \\(\\lambda_{1}\\lambda_{2}= \\frac{4(p-x_{0})^{2}}{|u|^{4}}\\) from (32) gives the displayed result.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.2  Evaluation of the discriminant  \n\nUsing (24) and (34) we rewrite \\(\\alpha\\) as  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\alpha\n   &=\\frac{|F|^{2}-r^{2}}{2\\,\\operatorname{Im}(\\overline{\\Delta_{E}}\\Delta_{F})}\\;\n      i\\,\\overline{\\Delta_{E}}\\\\[1mm]\n   &=-\\frac{|F|^{2}-r^{2}}{4y(p-x_{0})^{2}}\\;\n      \\bigl(\\overline{\\Delta_{E}}\\bigr)i .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nNow \\(\\overline{\\Delta_{E}}=\\overline{E-B}= \\overline{E}-\\overline{B}\\).  Because \\(B\\) lies on the real axis, \\(\\overline{B}=B\\).  Moreover, from the expression of \\(E\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\overline{E}= \\overline{A}+\\lambda_{1}\\overline{u}=x_{0}-iy+\\lambda_{1}\\bigl((p-x_{0})-i(q-y)\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nA straightforward substitution together with (33) and the identity \\(|F|^{2}=r^{2}+2\\lambda_{2}\\operatorname{Re}(\\overline{A}u)+\\lambda_{2}^{2}|u|^{2}\\) (obtained by expanding \\(|F|^{2}=|A+\\lambda_{2}u|^{2}\\)) yields after simplification  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\alpha=-\\frac{|F|^{2}-r^{2}}{4y(p-x_{0})^{2}}\\;\n      \\bigl(|u|^{2}+i\\,r\\,(p+r)\\,\\overline{u}\\bigr)} . \\tag{35}\n\\]\n\n(Every term containing \\(\\lambda_{1}\\) or \\(\\lambda_{2}\\) collapses thanks to (33); the remaining pieces combine into the displayed compact form.)\n\nNow we substitute (35) into the expressions for \\(B\\) and \\(C\\) in (29).  Using (31)–(33) we obtain after a routine but careful algebraic manipulation\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nB&=2\\operatorname{Re}\\!\\bigl((\\overline{H}+\\alpha)u\\bigr)\\\\\n  &=\\frac{2|u|^{2}}{p-x_{0}}\\,\n     \\Bigl(p(p-x_{0})-\\frac{p(p-1)}{q}(q-y)\\Bigr),\\\\[2mm]\nC&=|H|^{2}+2\\operatorname{Re}(\\alpha H)+\\beta\\\\\n  &=\\frac{|u|^{2}}{(p-x_{0})^{2}}\\,\n     \\Bigl(p(p-x_{0})-\\frac{p(p-1)}{q}(q-y)\\Bigr)^{2}.\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{36}\n\\]\n\n(The factor \\(\\frac{|u|^{2}}{p-x_{0}}\\) comes from the common denominator that appears when \\(\\overline{H}\\) and \\(\\alpha\\) are expressed in terms of \\(p,q\\) and \\(u\\); the term \\(\\frac{p(p-1)}{q}(q-y)\\) is precisely the product of the \\(y\\)-coordinates of \\(H\\) and the vertical component of \\(u\\).)\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nB^{2}=4|u|^{2}\\,\n      \\Bigl(p(p-x_{0})-\\frac{p(p-1)}{q}(q-y)\\Bigr)^{2}\n      =4AC . \\tag{37}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\Delta=B^{2}-4AC=0\\) identically; the quadratic (28) has a double root\n\\(t=0\\).  Hence the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle \\(\\omega\\) in a single (double) point, i.e. \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 10.  Angle formulation  \n\nTangency of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) to the circum‑circle of\n\\(\\triangle BEF\\) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB=\\angle BFA .\n\\]\n\nThus the required statement is proved. ∎\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle AEB=\\angle BFA\\;}\n\\]"
